Fix a 24-byte heap overflow that crashes the agent on every client steer.

THE SYMPTOM: EasyMesh client steering never worked. The controller sent a
Client Steering Request, the agent received it, acknowledged it, and no 802.11
BTM Request ever reached the client. It looked like a protocol or an RF problem
and was chased as both. It is neither -- the agent aborts.

    systemctl show em_agent -p NRestarts   ->  NRestarts=1
    /minidumps/onewifi_em_agen_*.dmp       (one per steer attempt)

    Crash reason: SIGABRT
    #2  em_agent_t::handle_btm_request_action_frame  em_agent.cpp:453
    #3  em_mgr_t::start                              em_mgr.cpp:557
    #4  main                                         em_agent.cpp:2501

    (analyze_btm_request_action_frame is inlined into its only caller.)

THE DEFECT: the buffer is used two different ways and is sized for the smaller.

'len' is the action payload -- category + bss_tm_req + one neighbour report --
which is what frame_len reports and what is memcpy'd down to frame_data at the
end of the function. But the payload is BUILT through an ieee80211_mgmt overlay
placed at frame_data, and u.action sits 24 bytes into that struct, after the
802.11 header (frame_control, duration, da, sa, bssid, seq_ctrl). Writing the
candidate list through the overlay therefore touches
offsetof(u.action.u.bss_tm_req.variable) + sizeof(em_80211_neighbor_report_t)
bytes, not 'len' bytes.

Measured on the target rather than derived by eye -- instrumentation printing
every size, offset and the resulting bound:

    sizes:   action_frame_params_t=22  ieee80211_mgmt=40  category=1
             bss_tm_req=6  neighbor_report=15  btm_req_var=0
    offsets: u.action.category=24  u.action.u.bss_tm_req=25
             u.action.u.bss_tm_req.variable=31
    ALLOC=44   LAST BYTE WRITTEN AT OFFSET=68   OVERFLOW=24

The 24-byte shortfall is exactly offsetof(u.action.category), i.e. the 802.11
header the allocation never accounted for.

WHY IT ABORTS RATHER THAN FAULTS: the overrun corrupts heap metadata past the
end of the chunk, which glibc does not notice until its next allocation. That
happens inside the very next em_printfout in the function, so the process dies
AT that log line -- which is why "STA MAC for BTM request" never appeared, and
why its absence was initially misread as the function never being entered.
SIGABRT, not SIGSEGV, for the same reason.

THE FIX: size the allocation for the overlay, taking the larger of the two uses.
'len', frame_len, the memcpy and raw_data_len are all left alone -- they are
correct for the frame that is actually transmitted. Raising 'len' instead would
have produced a malformed 46-byte action frame.

VERIFIED on a controller and its extender, one steer per deployment (steering is
one-shot per em_t, see doc/easymesh):

                                   before      after
    NRestarts                        1           0
    minidumps                        1           0
    "STA MAC for BTM request"        0           5
    "Frame subdoc send successfull"  0           5
    steering requests received       1           5

The receive count is the clearest tell: before the fix the agent crashed on the
first request and restarted, so the controller's four retries arrived at a dead
process. After it, all five are received and all five produce a frame pushed to
Device.WiFi.AccessPoint.1.RawFrame.Mgmt.Action.Tx.

Not gated behind HWSIM_RADIO. The arithmetic is platform-independent -- any
build on which u.action is offset into ieee80211_mgmt (i.e. all of them)
under-allocates by the header size and corrupts the heap on the first client
steer. Nothing about this is specific to mac80211_hwsim or to containers.

Upstream: unified-wifi-mesh v0.3.1 (SRCREV c0e72a3).

---
diff --git a/src/agent/dm_easy_mesh_agent.cpp b/src/agent/dm_easy_mesh_agent.cpp
index 3b14bee0..6df95b48 100644
--- a/src/agent/dm_easy_mesh_agent.cpp
+++ b/src/agent/dm_easy_mesh_agent.cpp
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@
